===Etymology 1===
[[Image:Tiled roof in Dubrovnik-edit.jpg|thumb]]
{{ja-kanjitab|かわら|yomi=kun|alt=𦨞:rare}}

{{IPAchar|/kapara/}} → {{IPAchar|/kaɸara/}} → {{IPAchar|/kawara/}}

Probably from {{der|ja|sa|sort=かわら|कपाल||cup, bowl, skull}},<ref name="DJR">{{R:Daijirin}}</ref><ref name="KDJ2">{{R:Nihon Kokugo Daijiten 2|瓦・𦨞}}</ref><ref name="SDHJ">{{R:Sekai Dai Hyakka Jiten}}</ref> possibly via {{der|ja|ltc|sort=かわら|-}} {{ltc-l|迦波羅|id=2,1,1}}. Along with Buddhism, roof tiles came to Japan in the {{w|Asuka period}}.<ref name="SDHJ"/><ref name="N">{{R:Nipponica}}</ref><ref name="BJ">{{R:Britannica Japan}}</ref>

Cognate with {{m|ja|骨}}, {{m|ja|䯊|tr=kawara||a bone, particularly a covering bone such as a skull or kneecap}}.

Folk etymologies include:
* From {{m|ja|変わる|変わら|tr=kawara}}, the {{m|ja|未然形|tr=mizenkei||incomplete form}} of verb {{m|ja|変わる|tr=kawaru||to change}}, from the idea that tiles were produced by changing the clay into pottery through [[firing]]

